# The map: the nodes and the shared places, drawn where they are rather than listed.
#
# What this scene is for is the d-pad. It is the only screen in the client where the four
# directions move the picture instead of a cursor, and that is not a thing a still can show - so
# the sequence below aims the crosshair, which is what "aiming is panning" looks like from the
# outside.
#
# Aiming is the word that had to be earned, and it was earned twice. A direction used to move the
# view by a fifth of the body, which meant the crosshair only ever landed on a lattice 176 across
# and 84 down while the crosshair itself captures 28 - so five markers in six could not be put
# under it at all, and the reader's remedy was to zoom in and out until one happened to line up.
# Then it aimed too well: a press went to the nearest marker in its quadrant however far away it
# was, so on a mesh of any density every tap handed the reader the next node and the ground
# between two of them could not be stood on at all.
#
# What a press is now is a *step* - about a fifth of the body, as the pan always was - which ends
# on a marker when there is one inside it. So the first half of this scene is aiming, each press
# landing and the line under the map filling in, and the last half is the other half: the same
# key over ground with nothing near enough on it, moving the world instead.
#
# The places are put at nodes that are already in the demo roster, so every distance on the panel
# is measured between two real fixes rather than between two numbers typed to agree - the same
# reason the Waypoints scene puts them there.

scene demo
waypoint ALFA Ridge repeater | up the fire road, gate code is on the tag
waypoint CHRL Water cache

# The first row of the Nodes list is the way in, and it says how many of the roster has a
# position at all - which on a real mesh is a small fraction of it.
tab nodes
hold 1000
key a
hold 1600

# The map opens framed on everything it can place, with the crosshair wherever that leaves it -
# which here is open grid between the cluster and the far node, so the line under the map says
# there is nothing under it and the crosshair is drawn dim.
#
# One press west and it is on ALFA. The view centres on the marker's own coordinates rather than
# sliding a fixed distance towards it, so the landing is exact: the node's name, how far it is
# from our own radio and which way, and how much of its position to believe.
key left
hold 1800

# One level closer. A zoom keeps the centre, so what was aimed at stays aimed at while the grid's
# spacing steps down the 1-2-5 ladder and the scale bar follows it.
key x
hold 1600

# East twice, marker to marker: our own radio, then FXTR beyond it. No key was spent to get this
# - it is the same four directions - and open grid still pans, which is what the fall-back is
# for and what a basemap will need.
key right
hold 1400
key right
hold 1600

# And opening what the crosshair is on: the node's own detail, over the map rather than instead
# of it - so B lands back on the view that was aimed rather than on the list.
key a
hold 1600
key b
hold 1200

# Back to everything, which is the view the map opened on. That is what makes the first press
# something a reader can undo.
key start
hold 1400

# And two levels closer than the fit, where the cluster is off the panel and the header says 0 of
# 24 - which is the other half of the rule. There is nothing within a step of the crosshair, so
# the presses below are pans: the grid moves under a crosshair that stays empty, which is what
# looking around between two nodes is. A press that went to the nearest marker however far away
# it was would have answered each of these by teleporting back onto the cluster.
key x 2
hold 1200
key left
hold 1200
key up
hold 1600
